          <a href="#which-one" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Which One Your Deck Needs</a>
          <a href="#lifespan" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Lifespan and Recoating</a>
          <a href="#sealer-wins" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Where Clear Sealer Wins</a>
          <a href="#ten-years" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. The Ten-Year Cost</a>
          <a href="#common-mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Common Mistakes to Avoid</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The question is framed as a choice between two products, and it very rarely is. Nearly every deck stain on the shelf already contains the water repellent that a sealer sells on its own, which means the real decision is not stain or sealer but how much pigment you are willing to put on the wood.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            A clear sealer stops liquid water soaking into the timber. A stain stops liquid water soaking into the timber <em>and</em> puts pigment in the way of ultraviolet light. Water causes cupping, checking, splitting and rot. Ultraviolet light causes the silvery grey. If you only stop the water, you get a deck that stays structurally sound while it goes grey anyway, which is exactly the outcome that surprises people a year after a weekend of work.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Rule Worth Remembering</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Water repellency is what a coating does. Pigment is what makes it last.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Sunlight breaks down both the wood surface and the binder holding the coating together. Pigment absorbs and scatters that light before it gets to either. This is why, on the same deck in the same climate, a clear coat needs redoing in a year and a solid coat is still working in four.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            For the overwhelming majority of decks, the correct answer is a pigmented stain. The only common situations where a clear sealer is the better call are covered in section seven, and they are narrower than the shelf space given to clear products suggests.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-they-do" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Each One Actually Does
          </h2>

          <p>
            Strip the marketing away and there are three functional ingredients in any deck finish, in various proportions.
          </p>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>A water repellent</strong>, usually a wax or a resin, which makes the surface shed liquid water rather than drink it.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>A binder</strong>, oil or acrylic, which carries everything into or onto the wood and holds it there.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Pigment</strong>, the finely ground solid that gives colour and, far more importantly, blocks ultraviolet light.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            A clear sealer is the first two without the third. A stain is all three. That is the whole distinction, and it explains why a product labelled "stain and sealer in one" is not a marketing trick — it is an accurate description of what nearly every exterior stain has always been. If you are choosing between them, our <a href="/best-deck-sealant/">deck sealant reviews</a> and <a href="/best-deck-stain-reviews/">deck stain reviews</a> cover the products at each end.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is a fourth ingredient worth knowing about, though it does not change the stain-versus-sealer question: a mildewcide. Most quality exterior finishes carry one, and on a shaded, damp deck it is doing quiet work that you only notice by its absence. It has nothing to do with pigment or repellency and does not survive as long as either.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="opacity-ladder" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Opacity Ladder
          </h2>

          <p>
            Rather than two categories, think of a single ladder running from no pigment to complete coverage. Every rung trades away grain for durability.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Pigment</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Life</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Clear sealer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1–2 years, and greys throughout</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Toner / transparent</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Trace</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1–2 years, slight colour hold</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Semi-transparent</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Light, grain fully visible</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2–3 years</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Semi-solid</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Heavy, grain texture only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3–4 years</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Solid / opaque</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Full, grain hidden</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3–5 years, can peel</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Deck paint</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Full, forms a film</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4–7 years, peels when it fails</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Those figures assume a horizontal, fully exposed deck surface, which is the harshest place a wood finish is ever asked to work. The same product on a vertical fence or a shaded railing routinely doubles the numbers, which is why a fence and a deck stained on the same weekend need attention years apart. Our <a href="/best-fence-stain/">fence stain guide</a> covers that case separately.
          </p>

          <p>
            Note where the failure mode changes. Everything down to semi-solid penetrates the wood and wears away, which means it fades rather than flakes and a recoat is mostly a matter of cleaning. Solid stain and paint sit on the surface as a film, which lasts longer but eventually peels, and peeling has to be stripped rather than washed. That trade is covered in depth in our comparison of <a href="/solid-vs-semi-transparent-deck-stain/">solid and semi-transparent stain</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pigment" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Why Pigment Decides Everything
          </h2>

          <p>
            Wood is roughly a quarter lignin, the natural polymer that binds the cellulose fibres together and gives timber its colour. Lignin is also unusually good at absorbing ultraviolet light, and when it does, it degrades. The surface layer of a deck left in full sun loses its lignin over a season or two, leaving loose grey cellulose fibres that wash off in rain and expose the next layer down. That is what greying is, and it is happening whether the deck is wet or dry.
          </p>

          <p>
            A clear coating cannot interrupt that process, because ultraviolet light passes straight through it. Worse, the same light that is destroying the lignin is also attacking the binder in the coating, so a clear finish on a sunny deck is being consumed from above and losing its grip from below at the same time. This is the honest reason clear sealers need annual attention while pigmented stains do not.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pigment particles interrupt this by absorbing or scattering the light before it reaches either the wood or the binder. More pigment means more interruption, which is why the durability column in the table above climbs so cleanly as opacity increases. It is not that solid stain is a better formula than semi-transparent; it is that it carries more of the ingredient that stops the sun.
          </p>

          <p>
            One practical consequence surprises people: darker does not automatically mean more protection. A dark pigment absorbs more heat, and a horizontal deck in full sun can reach surface temperatures that stress any coating and make the boards uncomfortable underfoot. Mid-tone pigments are usually the sweet spot for exposed decks, and our <a href="/deck-stain-colors-guide/">deck stain colours guide</a> covers how that plays out in practice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-one" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Which One Your Deck Needs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Match the answer to the wood rather than to the aisle.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Your Situation</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What to Use</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">New pressure-treated deck</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Semi-transparent stain, once the wood accepts water</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cedar or redwood you want to keep warm-toned</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Transparent or semi-transparent, recoated often</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Older deck, uneven colour, some grey</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Semi-solid or solid, which evens it out</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Deck already coated with a solid product</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stay solid, or strip completely first</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Tropical hardwood (ipe, cumaru)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Penetrating oil or a clear coat, recoated annually</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fully shaded, damp deck</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Any pigment level, but prioritise mildew resistance</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The pressure-treated case deserves an extra sentence, because it is the most common deck in the country and the most commonly mishandled. Treatment protects against rot and insects from the inside; it does nothing whatsoever about sun or surface water. New treated lumber is also frequently too wet to accept any finish, and applying one to saturated wood produces a blotchy, short-lived result no matter what you spent. Our guides to <a href="/can-you-stain-pressure-treated-wood/">staining pressure-treated wood</a> and <a href="/best-deck-stain-for-pressure-treated-wood/">the best stains for it</a> cover the timing test in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cedar and redwood pull in the opposite direction. Both are naturally beautiful and both grey just as readily as anything else, so the honest trade is between keeping the colour and keeping the schedule light. A transparent finish shows the wood off and asks for a recoat every year or two; a semi-transparent one holds the tone longer at the cost of some depth. See our guides to <a href="/best-deck-stain-for-cedar/">cedar stains</a> and <a href="/best-redwood-sealer/">redwood sealers</a> for how the species change the choice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lifespan" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Lifespan and the Recoating Reality
          </h2>

          <p>
            Manufacturer lifespans are printed for a reason and they are not dishonest, but they describe a best case: correct wood, correct preparation, correct weather, and a surface that gets some shade. Read them as ceilings rather than expectations. A deck in unbroken southern sun with a dog and a barbecue on it will not match the label, and that is not a product fault.
          </p>

          <p>
            What actually changes the number on a real deck is a short list. Sun exposure comes first by a wide margin. Preparation comes second, because a coating applied over a dirty or still-sealed surface never bonds properly and starts failing early regardless of quality. Third is film thickness, where more is not better: over-application leaves a surface layer that never fully cures and gets sticky, which our guide to <a href="/how-many-coats-of-deck-stain/">how many coats of deck stain</a> deals with directly.
          </p>

          <p>
            The recoat itself differs sharply between the two ends of the ladder, and this is the part worth thinking about before you buy. A worn semi-transparent stain is cleaned and recoated in a weekend with no stripping. A failed solid stain or paint has to be scraped and stripped first, which is genuinely unpleasant work and can take longer than the original job. Choosing a solid finish is choosing a longer interval and a harder eventual reset — see <a href="/how-to-strip-deck-stain/">stripping deck stain</a> for what that actually involves.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sealer-wins" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Where a Clear Sealer Genuinely Wins
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clear sealer has a real place, and it is worth being specific about where, because the advice to always use pigment is nearly right rather than entirely right.
          </p>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Dense tropical hardwoods.</strong> Ipe and its relatives are too dense to absorb much pigment, and their natural oils and extractives already resist rot. A penetrating clear or lightly tinted oil, refreshed annually, is the normal approach.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>You want the wood to grey deliberately.</strong> Coastal and modern designs often aim for silver, and a clear sealer lets the colour go while still keeping water out of the end grain.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Heavily shaded structures.</strong> Where UV is genuinely low, the pigment argument weakens and water and mildew resistance become the whole job.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>You genuinely will recoat every year.</strong> Annual clear coats keep a deck looking close to new. Most people do not sustain this, but if you will, it works.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            What links all four is honesty about the maintenance schedule. Clear finishes work when they are refreshed often, and disappoint when they are treated like a multi-year coating. Our <a href="/best-exterior-wood-sealer/">exterior wood sealer testing</a> goes into which clear products hold their repellency longest, and it is a real spread rather than a formality.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ten-years" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. The Ten-Year Cost, Which Is the Real Comparison
          </h2>

          <p>
            Per-can pricing makes clear sealer look cheap and solid stain look expensive, and over a decade the ranking often reverses. On a typical deck, a clear sealer wants doing roughly six to eight times in ten years, a semi-transparent stain three or four, and a solid stain two or three. Multiply the material by those numbers and the gap narrows sharply.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then add the labour, which is where the real cost sits for a homeowner. Every recoat is a full weekend: clear the furniture, wash the deck, wait for it to dry, apply, wait again. Six of those weekends is a meaningfully different life from three, and nothing about the cheaper can compensates for it. If you are paying someone, the arithmetic is even starker, because labour dominates the invoice on every visit regardless of what goes in the sprayer.
          </p>

          <p>
            The counterweight is the eventual strip. A solid finish that has been recoated twice will one day need taking back to bare wood, and that is a job with a real cost of its own. So the honest framing is not that pigment is cheaper, but that mid-ladder finishes — semi-transparent and semi-solid — tend to give the best total cost over a decade, because they last several years and still fail gracefully by wearing rather than peeling.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Reading a Label Without Being Misled</h2>
        <p>
          Once you know that pigment is the variable, product names stop being confusing. "Waterproofing wood stain" is a pigmented finish with a repellent, which is to say a normal deck stain. "Clear wood finish" or "water seal" is the repellent alone. "Toner" is a stain with a trace of pigment. "Semi-transparent" and "solid colour" describe how much. Nothing in the naming tells you about quality, only about opacity, and two products at the same rung can perform very differently.
        </p>
        <p>
          The number worth finding on the can is the coverage rate, given in square feet per gallon. A penetrating semi-transparent stain typically claims somewhere in the region of two hundred to four hundred square feet per gallon on smooth wood, and considerably less on rough or weathered timber that drinks more. A wildly high coverage claim usually signals a thin product, and a low one usually signals a heavy-bodied film former. Comparing coverage rates is a far better guide to what you are buying than comparing price per gallon.
        </p>
        <p>
          The second thing to check is whether the product is oil-based or water-based, because it determines cleanup, cure time, smell, and how the next recoat will behave. Neither is universally better and the modern water-based formulas have closed most of the historic gap, but the choice has consequences that last as long as the deck does. Our comparison of <a href="/oil-based-vs-water-based-deck-stain/">oil-based and water-based deck stain</a> covers where each still genuinely leads, and our <a href="/best-oil-based-deck-stain/">oil-based stain testing</a> covers the products.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Why the Preparation Matters More Than the Choice</h2>
        <p>
          It is worth saying plainly that the stain-versus-sealer decision matters less than what happens before either goes on. A mid-priced semi-transparent stain applied to a properly cleaned, brightened and dry deck will outlast a premium product applied over mill glaze, mildew or a still-working old sealer. Adhesion and penetration are decided entirely by the state of the wood at the moment of application, and no formula compensates for a surface that will not accept it.
        </p>
        <p>
          The single most useful diagnostic costs nothing. Sprinkle a little water on several parts of the deck and watch. If it beads and sits, something is still repelling it — old sealer, mill glaze on new lumber, or a factory coating — and any finish applied now will sit on the surface and dry blotchy. If it darkens the wood and soaks in within a minute or two, the deck is open and ready. Do this before buying anything, because the result may change what you buy.
        </p>
        <p>
          Beyond the water test, preparation means removing mildew and the grey surface fibres, neutralising any strong cleaner with a wood brightener, and letting the boards dry properly — usually two clear days, longer after a pressure wash. Our guides to <a href="/how-to-prep-a-deck-for-staining/">prepping a deck</a> and <a href="/best-deck-cleaner/">choosing a deck cleaner</a> cover the sequence, and the full job is laid out in <a href="/how-to-stain-a-deck/">how to stain a deck</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">What Happens If You Get It Wrong</h2>
        <p>
          Choosing a clear sealer when you wanted colour retention is the mildest mistake on the list. The deck greys under a working water repellent, you are disappointed at the end of the first summer, and the fix is a clean and a pigmented recoat. Nothing has to be stripped, because the sealer wears rather than peels. It costs a season and a weekend.
        </p>
        <p>
          Going the other way is more expensive. Putting a solid stain or paint on a deck that will be hard to strip later commits you to that decision more or less permanently, because a solid coating cannot be undone with a wash and going back to a semi-transparent look means taking every trace off. This is the one direction on the ladder that is genuinely difficult to reverse, which is why moving up it slowly — transparent, then semi-transparent, then semi-solid over the years — is a sound long-term approach.
        </p>
        <p>
          The most common failure of all is not a choice at all but a compatibility problem: applying a new finish over an old one that has not worn off or been removed. The new coat cannot penetrate, so it lies on top, dries unevenly, stays tacky in places, and lifts within a season. The water test catches this before it happens, and our guide to <a href="/can-you-stain-over-old-deck-stain/">staining over old deck stain</a> covers the cases where a recoat is legitimately fine and where it is not.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">A Sensible Default for Most Decks</h2>
        <p>
          If you want one recommendation to work from, it is this: on an exposed pressure-treated deck, a quality semi-transparent stain in a mid-tone, applied to properly prepared wood in settled weather, gives the best balance of appearance, lifespan and future flexibility that any product on the shelf can offer. It shows the grain, it carries enough pigment to genuinely slow the greying, it wears rather than peels, and it leaves every option open for the next coat in two or three years' time.
        </p>
        <p>
          Deviate from that default for a reason rather than out of habit. Go clearer for a hardwood or a deliberate silver finish, and accept the annual schedule that comes with it. Go more opaque for a tired deck with uneven colour or a previous solid coating, and accept that the eventual reset will be harder. Both are legitimate; what is not legitimate is choosing a clear sealer while expecting it to hold colour, which is the mismatch behind most of the disappointment in this category.
        </p>
        <p>
          Whatever you choose, the timing of the work matters nearly as much as the product. Wood needs to be dry through, temperatures need to be moderate, and the coating needs a rain-free window long enough to cure rather than merely dry. That is covered in <a href="/best-time-of-year-to-stain-a-deck/">the best time of year to stain a deck</a> and <a href="/deck-stain-drying-time/">deck stain drying times</a>, and getting it right costs nothing but patience.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting a Clear Sealer to Stop Greying</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                It cannot, because ultraviolet light passes straight through it and greying is UV damage to the wood surface. The sealer is doing its job while the deck turns silver. If colour retention is the goal, the product needs pigment in it, and the amount of pigment sets how long the colour lasts.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying a Clear Sealer Over a Fresh Stain</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Almost every deck stain already contains the repellent, so a clear topcoat adds nothing and risks a surface film that clouds, gets sticky, or peels off in sheets the following spring. Unless the stain manufacturer specifies a matched topcoat, the stain is the finished system.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Staining Over a Sealer That Still Works</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A working repellent stops the stain penetrating, so it sits on the surface, dries blotchy and wears off in patches. Sprinkle water on the boards first: beading means the old finish is still active and the deck needs stripping or more weathering before anything new goes on.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating New Pressure-Treated Lumber Immediately</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Fresh treated boards are often still saturated from the treatment process and will reject stain and sealer alike, producing a patchy finish that fails early. The water test is the only reliable readiness check; the calendar is not, because drying time depends entirely on climate and how the lumber was stored.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing on Price Per Can</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The cheapest product is usually the thinnest and needs redoing soonest, and the labour of an extra recoat weekend dwarfs the saving on the pail. Compare coverage rate and expected years of service instead, and count the total weekends over a decade rather than the price on the shelf.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between deck stain and deck sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A sealer is a clear water repellent. A stain is a water repellent carrying pigment. Both keep water out; only the pigmented one blocks ultraviolet light, which is what actually turns a deck grey. Almost every modern deck stain contains a sealer within it, so the choice is really about how much pigment you want.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to seal a deck after staining it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not. Nearly all exterior deck stains already contain the water repellent, so a clear sealer on top is redundant and can leave a film that clouds or peels. The exception is a few solid-colour systems that specify a matched clear topcoat. If the label does not call for one, do not add one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is clear deck sealer a waste of money?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No, but it is often misunderstood. It does one job well — stopping liquid water soaking into the wood — and nothing about UV, so the deck greys on schedule anyway. It makes sense on dense hardwood, on shaded structures, and where you genuinely intend to recoat every year.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does deck sealer last compared to stain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On an exposed horizontal deck, roughly one to two years for a clear sealer, two to three for semi-transparent, and three to five for solid. The pattern tracks pigment, because pigment shields both the wood and the coating's own binder from the sunlight that breaks them down.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you put stain over a deck that was sealed?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only once the sealer has worn off or been stripped. A working repellent stops the stain penetrating, so it dries blotchy and wears away in patches. Sprinkle water on the boards: if it beads, the sealer is still active and the deck is not ready for stain.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which is better for a pressure-treated deck, stain or sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Stain, in almost every case. Treatment protects against rot and insects but does nothing about UV or surface water, so treated lumber still checks, cups and greys. A semi-transparent stain supplies both the repellency and the pigment; a clear sealer supplies only half of it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does deck sealer stop the wood going grey?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Greying is ultraviolet damage to the lignin in the surface fibres, and a clear film has nothing in it to absorb or reflect UV. A deck under clear sealer stays water-resistant while it greys, which is exactly why the result surprises so many people.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should a new deck get sealer or stain first?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Whichever you choose, first check the wood is dry enough to accept it. New pressure-treated lumber is often still wet from the plant and will reject both. Sprinkle water on it: beading means wait, soaking in within a minute or two means the wood is ready.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

