        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Sealer and stain are not rival products; they are two points on one scale. A sealer is the water repellent on its own, a stain is the same repellent with pigment added, and the pigment is what blocks the ultraviolet light that greys wood and degrades the coating. That is why durability climbs so cleanly as opacity increases.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For most decks, and particularly for exposed pressure-treated lumber, a semi-transparent stain is the right default. It shows the grain, slows the greying, lasts two to three years, and wears away rather than peeling, which keeps the next recoat easy. Clear sealer earns its place on dense hardwoods, in deep shade, and where you will genuinely recoat annually.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Whichever you pick, the water test comes first. If the deck beads, nothing new will penetrate and the job will fail regardless of what you bought. If it drinks, you are ready — and at that point the preparation matters more than the label on the can.
          </p>
        </div>


